<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Latvian Soviet Socialist Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1940 CE – 1991 CE · capital Riga</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Soviet Latvia, annexed in 1940 and restored to independence in 1990-1991 after the mass movements of the Baltic Way era.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After a Soviet ultimatum in June 1940, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> occupied the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia</a>. A rigged election produced a parliament that requested admission to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and Latvia was annexed in August 1940. German occupation followed in 1941-1944, before Soviet rule returned.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Soviet power brought nationalisation, collectivisation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> in June 1941 and March 1949. Riga became a major industrial centre, producing radios, railway carriages and the RAF minibuses, which drew large numbers of Russian-speaking workers, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a> fell to barely half the population. Communist leaders included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arvids-pelse">Arvīds Pelše</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusts-voss">Augusts Voss</a>, and a 1959 purge removed national communists.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-of-latvia">Popular Front of Latvia</a> mobilised mass support, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-way">Baltic Way</a> human chain of 1989. On 4 May 1990 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/supreme-council-of-the-republic-of-latvia">Supreme Council</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolijs-gorbunovs">Anatolijs Gorbunovs</a> declared the restoration of independence, completed on 21 August 1991, restoring the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/latvian-soviet-socialist-republic.jpg" alt="Latvian Soviet Socialist Republic" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Denelson83, Urmas, Nokka, Great Brightstar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Latvian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic_(1953%E2%80%931990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1940 CE – 1991 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riga</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arvīds Pelše, Anatolijs Gorbunovs</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheran and Catholic Christianity (state atheism)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latvian, Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Latvia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Latvian SSR, Soviet Latvia</dd></dl></aside></div>
